--TEST--
Every binding site writes a type once — a local with and without an initializer, a `for` header's counter, a `foreach` key and value, a property, a parameter, a return and two `catch` bindings — `var` takes a local's type from its initializer exactly, and a name declared in one arm of an `if` is the same function-scoped binding the other arm assigns (`rule:types/declaration`, `rule:types/var-inference`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/every-binding-has-a-type

// `rule:types/declaration`: a local writes its type where it is declared, and
// a declaration with no initializer is filled in by a later bare assignment,
// which carries no type of its own.
int $count = 1;
string $label;
$label = "n=";
$count = $count + 1;
echo $label, $count, "\n";

// `rule:types/var-inference`: `var` takes the initializer's own checked type
// and fixes it there, so the binding is exactly as narrow as what fed it.
var $n = 41;
var $width = Core\Str::length("ab");
var $ratio = 0.5;
echo $n is int ? "int" : "?", " ", $width is uint ? "uint" : "?", " ",
     $ratio is float ? "float" : "?", "\n";

// `rule:types/declaration`: the declared type is fixed for the binding's whole
// life, so an assignment answers at the type the declaration gave it.
$ratio = $ratio * 2;
echo $ratio, " ", $ratio is float ? "float" : "?", "\n";

// `rule:types/declaration`: declaration is function-scoped -- a name written
// down in one arm of an `if` is the same binding the other arm assigns, and it
// is readable after the whole statement.
if ($count > 1) {
    string $verdict = "counted up";
} else {
    $verdict = "left alone";
}
echo $verdict, "\n";

// `rule:types/declaration`: three of the positions PHP has no slot for -- a
// `for` header's init clause and a `foreach`'s key and value -- each write one,
// and the counter outlives the loop it drove.
array<int> $xs = [10, 20, 30];
int $sum = 0;
for (uint $i = 0; $i < Core\Arr::count($xs); $i = $i + 1) {
    $sum = $sum + $xs[$i];
}
echo $i, " ", $sum, "\n";
foreach ($xs as string $k => int $v) {
    echo $k, "=", $v, ";";
}
echo "\n";

// `rule:types/declaration`: a property, a parameter and a return each write
// their own type, and a `catch` binding is a declaration as well, so two catch
// blocks in one function name two different variables.
class Box {
    public int $held = 0;
    public function fill(int $n): string {
        $this->held = $n;
        return "held";
    }
}
Box $box = new Box();
echo $box->fill(7), " ", $box->held, "\n";
try {
    throw new RuntimeError("first");
} catch (RuntimeError $first) {
    echo $first->message, ";";
}
try {
    throw new RuntimeError("second");
} catch (Throwable $second) {
    echo $second->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
n=2
int uint float
1 float
counted up
3 60
0=10;1=20;2=30;
held 7
first;second
